<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conrad of Montferrat</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1146–<a href="/years/1192">1192</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conrad of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> was an Italian nobleman who arrived at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 1187 and held it against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> after the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>. He became Marquis of Montferrat and, through marriage to Isabella, claimant to the crown of Jerusalem, and was elected king in April 1192. Days later he was murdered in Tyre by two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizari-ismailism">Nizari</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conrad-of-montferrat.jpg" alt="Conrad of Montferrat" width="320" height="571" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IsabelaKOnrad.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Conrad was born around 1146 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a>, then a margraviate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-italy">Kingdom of Italy</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, the second son of Marquess William V of Montferrat and Judith of Babenberg. Little is known of his early years, but from his twenties he was active in diplomacy and became an effective commander. After the family's alliance with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Comnenus</a>, he defeated Barbarossa's forces under Archbishop Christian of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mainz">Mainz</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camerino">Camerino</a> in September 1179 and took the chancellor hostage. In 1187 he married Theodora, sister of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-ii-angelos">Isaac II Angelos</a>, and received the rank of Caesar, but soon left for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Conrad arrived off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, which had fallen to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, and sailed north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, where he found the remnants of the crusader army after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Battle of Hattin</a>. With the support of the Italian merchant communities he reorganised the city's defences. Tyre withstood Saladin's siege. When Saladin returned in November 1187 he produced Conrad's captive father, William V, before the walls and offered to release him if Tyre surrendered; the old man told his son to stand firm, and William was later released unharmed. On 30 December Conrad's forces captured many Egyptian galleys.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guy-of-lusignan">Guy of Lusignan</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a> in 1189, Conrad refused him entry, saying that Guy had forfeited his rights at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-hattin">Hattin</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibylla-queen-of-jerusalem">Queen Sibylla</a> died of disease in 1190, her half-sister Isabella, the heiress, had her marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-iv-of-toron">Humphrey IV of Toron</a> annulled, and Conrad married her on 24 November 1190. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I of England</a> supported Guy, while Conrad had the backing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-france">Philip II of France</a>. Conrad inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montferrat">Montferrat</a> in 1191.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In April 1192 the barons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a> unanimously elected Conrad king. He was never crowned. On 28 April, riding home from a meal at the house of Philip, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beauvais">Beauvais</a>, he was stabbed by two men who turned out to be members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Order of Assassins</a>, a Nizari Isma'ili sect. His guards killed one and captured the other. He was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, and Isabella, pregnant, was married to Henry II of Champagne only seven days later.</p><p>The murder remains unsolved. The surviving assassin claimed under torture that Richard was behind it, which cannot be proved, and a letter attributed to the leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-assassins">Assassins</a> blamed a dispute over a captured ship but is believed to be forged. English-language fiction and film have mostly depicted Conrad as a villain, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-scott">Walter Scott</a>'s The Talisman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conrad of Montferrat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defeat at Hattin in 1187 cost it almost all its territory, though Tyre held out under <b>Conrad of Montferrat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Conrad of Montferrat</b>, who had held Tyre against Saladin after the fall of Jerusalem, was murdered in the city by two Nizari Assassins.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-28">28 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Conrad of Montferrat</b>, who had held Tyre against Saladin, married Isabella I of Jerusalem and so became de facto King of Jerusalem.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-24">24 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1187, when Saladin's victory left almost every other town lost, <b>Conrad of Montferrat</b> arrived and held Tyre against him, ruling it as his own base.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-tyre">Lordship of Tyre</a></li><li><blockquote>The Aleramici were famed crusaders: <b>Conrad of Montferrat</b> defended Tyre and was elected King of Jerusalem in 1192, and Boniface I led the Fourth Crusade and became King of Thessalonica.</blockquote><a href="/state/march-of-montferrat">March of Montferrat</a></li><li><blockquote>The Nizaris used targeted killings by devotees against powerful enemies, including <b>Conrad of Montferrat</b> in 1192, and twice attempted to kill Saladin.</blockquote><a href="/state/nizari-ismailis-of-syria">Nizari Ismailis of Syria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/casale-monferrato">Casale Monferrato</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in Tyre, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=45.13,8.46&amp;year=1175" aria-label="Casale Monferrato on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Conrad of Montferrat"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5508 6225h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5508 6225h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=45.13,8.46&amp;year=1175">What was here in 1175? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
